HOME_ROADMAP = [
("washing-machine-heavy-items", "guide",
"The washing-machine mistake that can cost more than a new machine",
"Washing rubber-backed mats, rugs and weighted blankets seems harmless — it's the load that most often ends a machine early.",
"""<p>Of all the things people ask a washing machine to do, the one that shortens its life fastest isn't overloading it with clothes. It's washing the heavy, water-hungry items most of us never think twice about: rubber-backed bath mats, large rugs, weighted blankets, memory-foam pillows. The machine accepts the load without complaint — and then quietly pays for it.</p>
<h2>Why a wet mat weighs like a box of books</h2>
<p>A bath mat is designed to absorb water, and it does the same job in the drum that it does on the bathroom floor. By the time it reaches the spin cycle it can hold many times its dry weight, and all of that weight sits in one clump on one side of the drum. A spin cycle is a fast, precise balancing act; a waterlogged mat turns it into a hammer. Repair technicians report the same story repeatedly — the strain lands on the drum bearings and the suspension, and a failed bearing is the classic "repair that costs more than the machine is worth" job: one US technician writing for Family Handyman puts a bearing fix around $500, with some mat-related repairs passing $1,000 once labour is counted.</p>
<h2>The second problem: the backing comes apart</h2>
<p>Rubber and latex backing doesn't survive machine washing gracefully. Heat, water and friction break it into flakes and crumbs that shed into the water, clog the pump filter, and lodge in the drain path. That's why a machine that "just washed a rug" often presents a week later as <a href="/home/washing-machine-wont-drain/">the machine that won't drain</a> — the two problems are frequently the same problem.</p>
<h2>Weighted blankets and foam: the same trap, heavier</h2>
<p>A weighted blanket is the extreme case — glass beads or metal shot, distributed unevenly as it shifts, holding water in every fold. Memory-foam pillows are the same: foam soaks water and never lets go of it in a spin. Neither is what the machine's designers modelled when they set the drum's balance limits, and both can walk a machine across the floor or stop the spin dead mid-cycle.</p>
<h2>What to do instead</h2>
<p>Check the care label first: most rubber-backed mats say hand-wash or spot-clean, and they mean it. Hand-wash mats in the tub with a brush, hang weighted blankets over two rails rather than one line, and sponge foam items instead of soaking them. If a label genuinely says machine-washable, add towels to balance the load, use the gentlest spin, and stop if the machine starts to thump — <a href="/home/how-to-clean-a-washing-machine/">a machine that's otherwise clean and well-maintained</a> tolerates an occasional borderline load far better than you'd think.</p>
<h2>When the damage is already done</h2>
<p>Loud rumbling that worsens on spin, or a drum that rocks by hand when the machine is off, points at the bearings — that's an engineer's job, not a DIY one, and worth a quote before you approve it. If the machine only thumps on heavy loads, start by cleaning the pump filter (most machines hide one behind a front flap) and check for backing debris. The wider lesson is the same one behind <a href="/home/mistakes/too-much-detergent/">the detergent overdose</a> and <a href="/home/mistakes/overloading-the-fridge/">an over-packed fridge</a>: appliances rarely die of one dramatic event — they die of loads they were never built for, repeated calmly for years.</p>
<p><em>Sources: US repair-technician reporting via Family Handyman (2025); manufacturer care-label guidance for rubber-backed mats and weighted textiles. Reviewed September 2026.</em></p>"""),

("dryer-lint-every-load", "guide",
"Why cleaning the dryer lint trap every load isn't optional",
"Lint is the most flammable thing in your home and it collects in the hottest place in it — the trap is a two-second habit with a fire-safety payoff.",
"""<p>Every few loads, someone decides the lint screen doesn't look that full and skips it. This is the single most expensive two-second skip in the laundry room, because lint isn't just a drying-efficiency problem — it's the fuel for one of the most common preventable house fires.</p>
<h2>What the fire data actually says</h2>
<p>The US Fire Administration's national study of clothes-dryer fires counted roughly 2,900 reported residential dryer fires a year in the US, causing tens of millions of dollars in property losses — and "failure to clean" was the leading contributing factor, accounting for about a third of them. Lint, dust and fibre were the items first ignited far more often than anything else. Lint is highly combustible by nature — it's fine, dry, fibrous material sitting directly in the hottest appliance in the house. A blocked trap also restricts airflow through the drum, which makes the machine run hotter, which is precisely the wrong direction.</p>
<h2>The habit, done properly</h2>
<p>Pull the screen before <em>every</em> load, not when it looks full — airflow matters long before the mesh visibly fills. Peel the lint off by hand rather than washing it, and occasionally wash the screen with warm soapy water: detergent residue and softener film build up invisibly on the mesh and choke airflow even when the screen looks clean. If water doesn't drain straight through the mesh, it needs that wash.</p>
<h2>The trap isn't the whole vent</h2>
<p>The screen catches most lint, but some always travels into the duct. Once or twice a year, check the vent duct where it leaves the machine and, ideally, where it exits the house — Clear the exterior flap of accumulated lint and any nesting material; a vent that's been choked for months quietly lengthens every drying cycle. While you're at it, confirm the duct is smooth rigid or semi-rigid metal, not the white plastic foil hose that older installs used — that material is a known fire risk and is worth replacing. Deep-vent work at height or inside walls belongs to a professional duct cleaner; the trap, the hose behind the machine and the exterior flap are the homeowner's territory and none of it needs tools beyond a screwdriver.</p>
<h2>Small signs worth respecting</h2>
<p> Clothes taking much longer to dry, a hot smell you can't place, the top of the machine running unusually warm, or the exterior vent flap barely moving on a cycle — all of these say airflow is restricted. Treat them the way you'd treat a smoke smell in a car: stop and look, not carry on. And never leave the house or go to sleep with the dryer running — the fires that do the damage are the unattended ones. This is the same logic as every other two-minute habit on this desk, from <a href="/home/how-to-clean-a-washing-machine/">cleaning the washing machine</a> to the <a href="/home/seasonal-home-maintenance-checklist/">once-a-season checklist</a>: homes fail slowly, then suddenly.</p>
<p><em>Sources: US Fire Administration, Clothes Dryer Fires in Residential Buildings (national NFIRS study); manufacturer dryer-manual maintenance guidance. Reviewed September 2026.</em></p>"""),

("dishwasher-loading-mistakes", "guide",
"The dishwasher loading mistakes that are quietly ruining your wash",
"Most 'my dishwasher doesn't clean well' complaints are loading problems — water can't clean what the spray can't reach.",
"""<p>A dishwasher is a rain system: it sprays water from below and between the racks, and anything that blocks or deflects that spray gets dirty dishes as a result. When a machine "stops cleaning well", the cause is far more often how it's loaded than how it's built — and the fixes below cost nothing.</p>
<h2>The big one: blocking the spray path</h2>
<p>Before you load, give the spray arms a spin by hand. Any plate, pan or tray that touches an arm will stop it or steal its rotation. Long cutting boards and sheet pans are the classic offenders laid flat on the bottom rack, where they act like a roof over everything beneath them. Tall items belong at the sides or back, sheets and boards belong on their edge in the top rack if they fit at all, and nothing should tower up into the top rack's path from below.</p>
<h2>Facing and spacing</h2>
<p>Dishes face the centre, where the spray is strongest — bowls, plates and containers angled inward and downward so water runs off rather than pooling. Overlapping is the quiet killer: two plates nested together look tidy and wash dirty on the facing surfaces, because a film of water can't reach a surface another dish is pressed against. Leave a visible gap between concave items. Spoons nested spoon-in-spoon wash each other instead of getting washed — mixed handles-down in a cutlery basket, laid flat in a drawer-style rack, and nothing small enough to fall through the basket floor loose.</p>
<h2>What goes where</h2>
<p>The top rack is for cups, glasses, and plastics that must stay clear of the heating element at the bottom — melted plastic on an element is an avoidable smell you only meet once. The bottom rack is for plates, pots and heavily soiled items that need the hottest, strongest spray. Sharp knives point down, and wooden items stay out entirely unless the label says dishwasher-safe — the heat cycle strips their finish the same way <a href="/home/vinegar-in-the-dishwasher/">the wrong cleaner strips a machine's seals</a>.</p>
<h2>The two checks that catch everything else</h2>
<p>First, the filter: a dishwasher's filter at the bottom of the tub needs a rinse under the tap every week or two. A clogged filter recirculates the very soil you're paying to remove, and no loading discipline survives it. Second, don't block the detergent door — an oversized pan in front of the dispenser can shield it from opening, so one item gets washed and everything gets a soap film. If you inherit a machine whose habits you don't know, run it empty on a hot cycle with a proper machine cleaner rather than reaching for vinegar — <a href="/home/stop-pre-rinsing-dishes/">and while you're recalibrating, check whether pre-rinsing is working against you too</a>. The deeper pattern is worth naming: appliance "failures" that are really technique, like <a href="/home/fridge-not-cold-enough/">a fridge that runs warm because air can't circulate</a>.</p>
<p><em>Reviewed September 2026 · general information, not professional advice.</em></p>"""),

("stop-pre-rinsing-dishes", "guide",
"Why pre-rinsing dishes before the dishwasher backfires",
"Modern detergent is built to attack food — rinse it all away first and you give it nothing to do except slowly etch your glassware.",
"""<p>For decades the polite thing was to rinse dishes almost clean before loading them. Modern machines and detergents flipped that advice — today, thorough pre-rinsing doesn't just waste water and minutes, it actively makes the wash worse.</p>
<h2>The enzymes need something to eat</h2>
<p>Modern dishwasher detergents work by enzyme action: proteins designed to break down starches and food soils during the wash. Enzymes latch onto actual food residue — give them a spotless plate and they have nothing to work on. That sounds harmless until you realise where the chemistry goes instead: with no soil to bind to, detergent particles stay suspended in the wash water at full strength, and the excess is a known contributor to etching — the permanent cloudy film on glassware that no amount of re-washing removes. Manufacturers have been saying "scrape, don't rinse" for years; the habit just hasn't caught up.</p>
<h2>What machines expect</h2>
<p>Dishwashers built in the last couple of decades are engineered around the assumption of scraped-not-rinsed dishes: soil sensors meter the cycle length and water use based on how dirty the first rinse water is. Rinse everything first and the sensor reads an easy load, shortening the cycle — so lightly soiled dishes get less wash, not more, than they would have. The machine optimises for a load you've already un-made.</p>
<h2>The right amount of prep</h2>
<p>Scrape bones, pits, and solid scraps into the bin — anything bigger than a coffee ground belongs in the trash, not the filter. Leave the thin film of sauce, egg, or oatmeal exactly where it is. The only exceptions worth respecting: burnt-on pans (soak those by hand), and anything that's been sitting for days, where a quick swill stops the machine smelling like a bin — the same airflow-and-residue logic as <a href="/home/how-to-clean-a-washing-machine/">a washing machine that smells</a>.</p>
<h2>Why the habit survives</h2>
<p>Pre-rinsing persists because it's inherited — people load the way the family machine from twenty years ago needed, when weaker detergents and coarser filters genuinely demanded it. If your current machine genuinely can't handle a scraped-only load, that's worth diagnosing rather than compensating for: check the filter first, then <a href="/home/dishwasher-loading-mistakes/">the loading patterns that block the spray</a>, and only then the machine itself. The pattern repeats across this desk — from <a href="/home/mistakes/too-much-detergent/">extra detergent</a> to <a href="/home/mistakes/streaky-windows-sunlight/">window-cleaning in sunlight</a>, the techniques that feel most diligent are often the ones quietly working against the machine.</p>
<p><em>Reviewed September 2026 · general information based on manufacturer guidance for modern dishwashers and detergents.</em></p>"""),

("vinegar-in-the-dishwasher", "guide",
"Stop defaulting to vinegar when you clean the dishwasher",
"Vinegar is the internet's favourite cleaner — but several dishwasher makers specifically warn that the acid attacks the machine's rubber.",
"""<p>Search for how to clean a dishwasher and the top answer is always a cup of white vinegar on the top rack. It genuinely does dissolve limescale — but it's also the one cleaner that several dishwasher manufacturers specifically warn against, and the reason is sitting in your machine's door.</p>
<h2>The acid and the rubber</h2>
<p>A dishwasher is a gasket machine: rubber or silicone seals around the door, the pump, the spray-arm bearings and the hoses. Vinegar's active ingredient is acetic acid, and Bosch and Electrolux are among the manufacturers who warn that repeated acid exposure degrades the rubber compounds in their machines — the seals stiffen, crack or swell, and a failed door gasket or pump seal leaks. As one chemist put it in Consumer Reports' coverage of vinegar cleaning: there are dozens of rubber formulations, some react with vinegar and some don't, and unless your manual says vinegar is fine, you're running an experiment on your own appliance.</p>
<h2>Frequency is the whole difference</h2>
<p>An occasional vinegar rinse is very unlikely to hurt a machine whose manual doesn't forbid it. The damage case is cumulative — the "clean it weekly with vinegar" routine, vinegar left in as a soak overnight, or vinegar substituted for rinse aid in the dispenser, which sits concentrated against the dispenser's seals between cycles. If you wouldn't run the experiment on a £600 machine, don't run it monthly.</p>
<h2>What to use instead</h2>
<p>For routine care, the machine's own heat does most of the work: run it hot and empty, with a dedicated dishwasher cleaner or a dishwasher-safe cleaner tablet, every month or two. For the parts that actually collect grime, nothing beats mechanical cleaning — <a href="/home/dishwasher-loading-mistakes/">rinse the filter under the tap weekly</a>, wipe the door seal lip and the spray-arm holes with an old toothbrush, and clear the arm jets with a cocktail stick. Hard-water scale on the tub wipes off with a paste of bicarbonate of soda, which is mildly alkaline and gasket-safe.</p>
<h2>The same rule everywhere in the house</h2>
<p>Vinegar's reputation comes from being brilliant on mineral scale — kettles, shower heads, glass. The lesson isn't "vinegar bad"; it's that acids and rubbers are a bad pairing anywhere in the home. It's the same reason the cleaning-safety shelf exists: <a href="/home/mistakes/mixing-cleaning-products/">never mix cleaning products</a>, <a href="/home/mistakes/too-much-detergent/">more detergent isn't more clean</a>, and <a href="/home/how-to-clean-a-washing-machine/">washing machines deserve the same gasket respect</a> — the internet's vinegar-and-bicarbonate routines are hard on front-loader door boots for exactly the same reason.</p>
<p><em>Sources: Bosch and Electrolux cleaning guidance as reported by Consumer Reports (2020); InSinkErator and manufacturer care documentation for adjacent appliances. Reviewed September 2026.</em></p>"""),

("fridge-coils-twice-a-year", "guide",
"Fridge coils: the twice-a-year habit that prevents early compressor failure",
"The condenser coils are the fridge's radiator. When they're carpeted in dust, the compressor runs hot — for years.",
"""<p>A refrigerator is the one appliance in your home that runs twenty-four hours a day, every day, for its whole life. The part that decides whether it does that easily or under strain isn't the thermostat or the door — it's the condenser coils, and almost nobody cleans them.</p>
<h2>What the coils actually do</h2>
<p>Your fridge doesn't "make cold" — it moves heat out of the box and dumps it into your kitchen through the condenser coils, usually on the back or underneath behind a toe-kick grille. Those coils are a radiator, and a radiator works by contact with air. When they're furred over with dust and pet hair, the insulation works both ways: heat can't escape, the compressor has to run longer and hotter to hit the same temperature, and it does that for years. That sustained heat is exactly what wears compressors out before their time — and a compressor job on a sealed system is the repair that usually retires the fridge, because it approaches the price of a new machine.</p>
<h2>The habit, twice a year</h2>
<p>Efficiency guidance across manufacturers and energy agencies converges on the same schedule: clean the coils at least twice a year, and every three to four months if you have shedding pets or a dusty kitchen — pet hair is precisely the right fibre length to mat into coil fins. Unplug or switch off the machine first. Pull it from the wall or pop off the front grille, brush the coils with a coil-cleaning brush or the vacuum's brush attachment — gently, the fins bend easily — vacuum what falls, and push it back. Ten minutes, twice a year, no tools beyond a vacuum.</p>
<h2>The signs you've left it too long</h2>
<p>The machine runs almost constantly, the sides near the coils feel hotter than they used to, the kitchen feels warmer in a way you can't attribute to weather, or the fridge struggles to hold temperature at all — at which point read <a href="/home/fridge-not-cold-enough/">the full warm-fridge checklist</a>, because coils are only one of its causes. A fridge that has to work this hard is also quietly running up the electricity bill, since it's typically the single biggest energy user in the kitchen.</p>
<h2>Pair it with the other six-month jobs</h2>
<p>While you're behind the machine, do its neighbours on the same schedule: the <a href="/home/fridge-door-seal-test/">dollar-bill test on the door seal</a>, a wipe of the gasket with warm soapy water, and a quick check that the drain hole inside the cabinet is clear. It's the same philosophy as the <a href="/home/seasonal-home-maintenance-checklist/">once-a-season checklist</a> and the opposite of <a href="/home/mistakes/overloading-the-fridge/">packing the fridge solid</a> — the appliance's job gets easier when you stop making it harder.</p>
<p><em>Sources: manufacturer maintenance documentation and energy-efficiency agency guidance (clean at least twice yearly; quarterly with pets). Reviewed September 2026.</em></p>"""),

("fridge-door-seal-test", "guide",
"The dollar-bill test: how to tell if your fridge door seal has failed",
"A gasket that leaks quietly wastes power and short-cycles the compressor all day — and the test takes thirty seconds.",
"""<p>The rubber gasket around a fridge door does one of the least glamorous jobs in the kitchen and one of the most consequential: it's the only thing standing between a sealed cold box and a warm room. When it fails, it fails quietly — no alarm, just a compressor that runs more than it should and a fridge that's always slightly working harder. The classic check costs thirty seconds and one banknote.</p>
<h2>The test</h2>
<p>Close the door on a piece of paper money — or a sheet of paper — so half sticks out. Pull gently. If you feel resistance and the note drags, the seal is gripping at that spot. If it slides out like the door is barely closed, that section of gasket has lost its grip. Work your way around both doors — the hinge side and the bottom corner are where gaskets usually give up first. Do the same spot-check on the freezer door while you're there.</p>
<h2>Before you buy a new gasket</h2>
<p>A weak spot often isn't a worn seal but a dirty or misplaced one. First, clean the gasket all the way into its folds with warm soapy water and a soft brush — a film of grease or sticky residue is enough to kill the grip, and it's the same residue that <a href="/home/fridge-coils-twice-a-year/">you're already wiping down on the six-month schedule</a>. Second, look at the door's hang: a fridge that's tilted slightly forward lets the doors swing open a fraction and never quite seat. Most fridges level with adjustable feet or front rollers; aim for a hair of backward tilt so doors fall closed. Third, check for folds: a gasket lifted out of its channel in one corner can often be pressed or warmed back into shape.</p>
<h2>When it really is the seal</h2>
<p>Gaskets crack with age, split at the corners, and go flat where they've been pinned open by a shelf for years. Visible cracks, torn sections, or a note that slides out all the way round mean replacement — and this is a genuinely homeowner-doable job on most machines: gaskets are orderable by model number, usually push or screw into a channel, and the new one seats better if you warm it first (a hairdryer on low relaxes the folds). If the model is old enough that the gasket is discontinued or the doors themselves are misaligned, weigh the repair against the machine's age the way you would with any appliance.</p>
<h2>Why it's worth the thirty seconds</h2>
<p>A leaking seal shows up twice on your bill: warm air leaks in, the compressor short-cycles to compensate, and cold air spills out every time the door fights the seal and reopens. It compounds with everything else that makes a fridge work hard — <a href="/home/mistakes/overloading-the-fridge/">shelves packed solid</a>, coils carpeted in dust, or a machine that can't hold temperature for any of the reasons in <a href="/home/fridge-not-cold-enough/">the warm-fridge guide</a>. Two of the fridge's three biggest enemies are invisible; the seal is the one you can check in under a minute.</p>
<p><em>Reviewed September 2026 · general information, not professional advice.</em></p>"""),

("garbage-disposal-mistakes", "guide",
"The garbage-disposal mistakes behind most service calls",
"Grease down the drain, hot water, and fibrous scraps account for most jammed and blocked disposals — and all three are habits.",
"""<p>The garbage disposal is a genuinely forgiving appliance — its maker's guidance is that most everyday food waste is fine. What kills it isn't exotic. It's three habits, repeated: grease down the drain, hot water instead of cold, and treating it like a bin for fibrous and starchy scraps. None of the three is dangerous to you; all three are expensive for the plumbing.</p>
<h2>Habit one: grease and fat</h2>
<p>Pour hot bacon fat down a running disposal and it vanishes — down the pipe, where it cools and hardens into a wax lining the drain. Do that weekly and the pipe's bore narrows until anything slow-moving sticks. InSinkErator's own guidance is blunt: never pour grease or fat into the disposal or any drain. Wipe greasy pans with a paper towel first, collect cooking fat in a jar or tin, and bin it once it sets. The same rule covers cream sauces and milk-heavy soups that behave like grease once they cool.</p>
<h2>Habit two: hot water at the wrong time</h2>
<p>Hot water feels like the cleaning choice, and it's exactly backwards here: it melts grease <em>on the way down</em> so it can solidify further along the pipe where it's harder to reach. The manufacturer's instruction is cold water, always — before you start the machine, the whole time it runs, and for a few seconds after. Cold water keeps any fat in the waste firm until it's ground and flushed, instead of painting the pipes with it.</p>
<h2>Habit three: the wrong scraps</h2>
<p>Fibrous vegetables — celery, asparagus, corn husks, onion skins — wrap around the grind ring and stall the motor; feed them in tiny quantities or compost them. Starchy expanders — pasta, rice, bread, potato peelings in bulk — turn to paste and settle into the trap. Eggshells and coffee grounds aren't the taboo the internet says (the manufacturer says both are fine in moderation), but they're sedimentary: they need a strong flow of cold water to actually leave the pipes. Feed everything in small batches with the water running hard — a disposal is a grinder, not a blender you can stuff.</p>
<h2>When it jams or blocks</h2>
<p>A humming disposal that doesn't grind has a jam: switch it off at the wall, then free the underside hex socket with the disposal wrench that came with it (or a 6mm Allen key), working the grind plate back and forth. Never put your hand in the chamber — that rule has no exceptions. A disposal that's completely silent usually just tripped its overload; the reset button is on the bottom of the unit. Water backing into the sink points further downstream — that's <a href="/home/how-to-unblock-a-toilet/">drain-blockage territory</a>, and the same physics as <a href="/home/how-to-fix-a-slow-draining-sink/">the slow-draining sink</a>, one appliance closer to the street. Recurring blockages, bad smells that survive cleaning, or a leak under the sink are plumber jobs — and if your home doesn't have a disposal at all, that's normal in the UK and much of Europe, where they're rare by plumbing culture and pipe design, not by accident.</p>
<p><em>Sources: InSinkErator official care and use guidance (support.insinkerator.com). Reviewed September 2026.</em></p>"""),

("induction-hob-wiring", "guide",
"Induction hobs: the hidden wiring upgrade nobody mentions",
"The pan technology gets all the attention — the real cost of going induction hides in your kitchen's wiring, and it's an electrician's question.",
"""<p>Induction is the best cooking technology most kitchens can't immediately plug in. The pans, the speed, the wipe-clean surface — all true. But an induction hob is also the most power-hungry appliance in the house, and the sticker price never includes what your wiring might need before the new hob boils a kettle of water. This page won't tell you how to do any of that work — it tells you why the quote surprises people, so the surprise happens before you buy, not after.</p>
<h2>Why induction is different from every hob before it</h2>
<p>A gas hob draws almost nothing electrically; a ceramic electric hob spreads its load across two or three heating zones that share the supply. A full-size induction hob concentrates several kilowatts behind one connection — typically needing its own dedicated high-current circuit, commonly a 32-amp circuit in UK terms or a 40-50 amp breaker in US terms, sized and protected for the specific model. Many kitchens already have that circuit from an old electric hob; many don't, especially where a gas hob was removed or a flat was wired for a plug-in countertop setup.</p>
<h2>The upgrade cascade</h2>
<p>When the circuit isn't there, the work isn't one job. Running a new circuit from the consumer unit (UK) or breaker panel (US) through walls and under floors; space in the consumer unit itself, which in older homes may mean a replacement unit; sometimes a supply-capacity conversation with the utility if the house total is marginal. Each step is legitimate, and none of it is DIY — new circuits and consumer-unit work are legally notifiable in the UK and belong to a qualified electrician everywhere, full stop. That's the same boundary this desk draws everywhere: <a href="/home/why-does-my-circuit-breaker-keep-tripping/">understanding your breaker</a> is homeowner territory; moving one is not.</p>
<h2>What to do before you buy</h2>
<p>Ask an electrician for a ten-minute look before you order the hob: is there an existing adequate circuit, what would a new one take in this house, and is there consumer-unit headroom. Get the wiring quote separately from the appliance purchase, and treat any total that includes consumer-unit replacement as a project quote, not a hassle surcharge. If you're renovating the kitchen anyway, this is the moment — circuit work through open walls costs a fraction of the same work through closed ones. It's the <a href="/home/mistakes/painting-without-prep/">prep-before-paint</a> principle at a thousand times the scale.</p>
<h2>One more check people forget</h2>
<p>Induction needs the right pans — magnet sticks to the base, or it doesn't heat at all — and it needs them across enough of the zone to register. Check your existing pans with a fridge magnet before assuming they'll need replacing too; many stainless sets are already induction-ready, which is usually the cheaper surprise. The wiring, though, is the part that turns a one-day swap into a two-week project, and it's the part the showroom never mentions.</p>
<p><em>Reviewed September 2026 · general information. Circuit installation and consumer-unit work must be done and certified by a qualified electrician.</em></p>"""),

("smart-appliances-worth-it", "guide",
"Smart appliances: when the 'smart' features are actually worth it",
"Most connected-appliance features are marketing gravity. A few genuinely earn their premium — here's the honest sorting.",
"""<p>Every appliance now comes in a "smart" version that costs more, needs an app, and promises a smarter home. Some of that money is well spent. Most of it buys features you'll use twice. This is the honest sort — not which brand to buy, but which capabilities change how an appliance serves you and which are screens bolted to a motor.</p>
<h2>The features that earn their keep</h2>
<p><strong>Remote start and stop</strong> is the genuine article for dishwashers and washing machines: run the machine on cheap overnight tariffs or have it finish as you get home, without leaving wet loads sitting all day. <strong>Push notifications at cycle end</strong> sounds trivial and isn't — a dryer that can tell you it's done stops wrinkles and re-runs. <strong>Diagnostics</strong> is the sleeper feature: machines that report their own fault codes let an engineer arrive with the right part, which can turn a two-visit repair into one — the same spirit as knowing the difference between a jam you can clear and a bearing an engineer must, from <a href="/home/washing-machine-heavy-items/">the load that wrote off a machine</a>. For fridges, <strong>temperature alerts</strong> — a warning that the freezer is warming while you're away — protect more value than any other single notification in the house.</p>
<h2>The features that rarely do</h2>
<p>Screens on fridges, cameras you check from the shop aisle, recipe apps on an oven, "smart" everything for its own sake. Each adds a component that can fail and a software layer that can outlive its usefulness. An appliance lasts ten to twenty years; app support and update commitments don't reliably stretch that far, which means the smart layer can quietly become the first dead feature — and on some machines, a control-board appliance is harder to use with the smart layer broken than a dumb one ever was. Before paying for connected features on a big purchase, assume a realistic question: will the manufacturer still care for this app in year twelve?</p>
<h2>The three-question test</h2>
<p>One: does this feature save me from being physically present, or just give me something to check on my phone? (The first is a feature; the second is a chore with notifications.) Two: does it keep working if the Wi-Fi drops? The best implementations run locally. Three: is the dumb version of the same machine noticeably worse at its actual job — cleaning, cooling, drying? If not, you're paying for the badge. A brilliant dumb fridge with clean <a href="/home/fridge-coils-twice-a-year/">coils and a healthy seal</a> beats a neglected smart one every time, and a machine of any generation washes better loaded properly — <a href="/home/dishwasher-loading-mistakes/">that discipline predates Wi-Fi</a>.</p>
<p>The honest summary: pay for remote start, alerts, and diagnostics; smile politely at the screen in the door.</p>
<p><em>Reviewed September 2026 · opinion and analysis, offered as such.</em></p>"""),
]
